WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.091 --> 00:00:01.630
(birds chirping)

00:00:01.630 --> 00:00:04.030
- [Narrator] The lei is a
quintessential part of Hawaiʻi,

00:00:04.030 --> 00:00:06.700
a symbol stretching back to ancient times.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:09.640
But lei are not one size fits all;

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:11.200
and across the Hawaiian Islands,

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.303
there are many lei makers
and many style of lei.

00:00:21.813 --> 00:00:24.500
(soft piano music)

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:25.824
This is Bill Char-

00:00:25.824 --> 00:00:28.593
a legend in traditional
Hawaiian lei making.

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:34.970
- The early people did very simple leis.

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:38.830
They weren't these gorgeous, colorful leis

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:40.970
like this today.

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:44.410
This is just done with ferns.

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:48.020
It's one object just braided on itself.

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:49.630
A lot of 'em were very simple,

00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:54.630
like tea leaf lei, used
for symbolic purposes.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.210
When you're making a lei,

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:02.580
and it's like a backbone,

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:05.350
your family ties.

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:07.940
And, you're adding each flower,

00:01:09.093 --> 00:01:11.850
it's sort of like a person, your cousin,

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:14.240
your sister, your auntie, your brother.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:19.240
All along, you're wrapping
on top of the stems.

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:22.290
That is the backbone of your family,

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:24.920
and it just keeps going on and on.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:26.253
Then you have your lei.

00:01:27.648 --> 00:01:29.330
(Hawaiian music)

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:31.060
Cruise lines would come in.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:33.850
Those are the old days

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:37.370
and people would come and
they would get their leis.

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:41.780
And it was part of the
tradition to stop in Hawaiʻi.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:44.023
When you stop, you got a lei.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:47.360
- [Narrator] When tourists
began coming to the islands,

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:50.770
a sewn style of lei
called Kui became popular,

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:53.530
and Chinatown was the place to get them.

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:55.480
One of the many places in Chinatown

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:58.290
is called Cindy's Lei and Flower Shop.

00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:00.460
It's been in business for 50 years.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:02.510
- In the very beginning,
there were maybe like four,

00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:03.990
four, five different type of leis.

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:06.280
Now, you have like 40-40 or more.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:08.540
Fifty years ago, flowers
are still being made

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:11.040
the same way-needle and string.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:13.850
The flowers get delivered
here, and we sort them out,

00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:16.600
and we string them the best we can.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:19.770
We craft so that it looks natural, open.

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:21.170
- [Narrator] Lei making is passed down

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:22.310
through family tradition,

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:24.540
and continues to find new expression

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:26.610
in the next generation.

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:29.320
- My tutu, or grandmother-and
she was a pretty

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:30.960
prolific lei maker in Hawaii-

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:32.390
I helped her make lei.

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:34.540
I sat there and cleaned her flowers.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:36.440
And I just got to sort of be immersed

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:38.723
in her passion of lei making.

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:41.730
There's many different styles of leis.

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:44.720
I'm, personally, specialized
in making Haku lei.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:48.100
A Haku lei is generally
worn on your head, lei po'o.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:49.490
Like I'm wearing mine on my head.

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:51.270
Some people wear them around their neck.

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:54.440
And Haku just refers to the
traditional style of making it.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:55.453
Here we go.

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:59.030
They have lots of different materials.

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:01.310
And then usually, in
Hawaiʻi, you don't show up

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:02.300
to a birthday party,

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:03.800
you don't show up to a wedding,

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:05.570
you don't show up to a baby shower

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:06.920
without a lei for somebody.

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:12.700
- A lei represents you.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:17.130
You don't take your lei
off because your mana,

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:20.883
your spirit is already part of this lei.

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:26.120
- I think that's one
of the best ways to say

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:27.890
a gift from the heart:

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:32.890
floral, sweet, tactile,
visual and very special.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:36.350
You know that if you got a
lei or if you gifted a lei,

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:38.050
you have a connection with Hawaiʻi.

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:41.070
- [Meleana] Lei, in
Hawaiʻi, is the ultimate

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:42.670
expression of love.

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:45.210
Hawaiians, we have an innate aloha spirit,

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:48.130
you know, which is a warm,
welcoming, stay with me,

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:50.360
eat with me, share with me,

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:52.660
and I think that is sort
of embodied in a lei.

00:03:58.851 --> 00:04:01.018
(chiming)

